e but unproven)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Myeloproliferative neoplasm (elevated white count with normal differential aligns with CML chronic phase but lacks blasts)", "Reactive leukemoid reaction (could explain elevated count but no evidence of chronic constitutional symptoms in this context)", "Atypical infection with granulocytic response (high WBC + normal differential is less typical than sepsis profiles)"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional features (weight loss, fatigue)", "Elevated WBC count (66.5 × 10⁹/L) with no neutrophilic predominance", "Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase elevation (Tier 2 high-risk evidence item)", "Mild hypertension (150/100 mmHg)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.7058823529411764, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Elevated LAP with normal differential (no blasts) is a key point favoring CML over reactive leukemoid reaction. CML classically demonstrates low LAP (Ph-negative) with blast phase showing increased blasts; reactive causes have elevated LAP.", "Normal exam without splenomegaly doesn't exclude CML, which can present without abdominal organomegaly in early phases. Constitutional symptoms alone justify the current branch assignment, though splenomegaly would strengthen it clinically."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of gradual blood count abnormalities", "Presence of splenomegaly or hepatomegaly"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Bone marrow biopsy with cytogenetics"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.9, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.196078431372549, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Normal differential (no lymphocytosis) reduces the likelihood of CLL or lymphoplasia, though MM remains plausible with non-specific leukocytosis. Lymphocytosis is more directly characteristic of lymphoproliferative disorders."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.0980392156862745, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Chronic symptoms (weight loss over time) contradict reactive leukemoid reaction caused by acute viral illness. While viral infections can cause transient reactive leukocytosis, the prolonged constitutional symptoms suggest myeloproliferative rather than reactive etiology."]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Chronic Phase", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.49411764705882344, "posterior": 0.49411764705882344, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of progressive leukocytosis without prior myeloproliferative disorder", "Absence of splenomegaly (can occur in early CML chronic phase)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Ph chromosome detection by FISH or molecular testing", "Bone marrow cytochemistry for myeloperoxidase (normal in CML chronic phase)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Myeloproliferative Neoplasm Not Otherwise Specified (MPN-NOS)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2117647058823529, "posterior": 0.2117647058823529, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Absence of JAK2/V617F mutation (more common in PV/ET)", "Normal red cell mass (contrasts with PV)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Bone marrow biopsy for fibrosis (excludes myelofibrosis)", "CSF3R mutation testing"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology"}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "In a 59-year-old man with constitutional symptoms (fatigue, weight loss), does the absence of blasts in the normal differential combined with elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) distinguish chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) from leukemoid reaction?", "result_summary": "Elevated LAP with normal differential (no blasts) is a key point favoring CML over reactive leukemoid reaction. CML classically demonstrates low LAP (Ph-negative) with blast phase showing increased blasts; reactive causes have elevated LAP.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "strong_for", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "strong_against"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Can the presence of normal differential (no lymphocytosis) in a 59-year-old with constitutional symptoms rule out lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorders like chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) or multiple myeloma?", "result_summary": "Normal differential (no lymphocytosis) reduces the likelihood of CLL or lymphoplasia, though MM remains plausible with non-specific leukocytosis. Lymphocytosis is more directly characteristic of lymphoproliferative disorders.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "moderate_against", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the history of a recent cold as a systemic viral infection justify reactive leukocytosis in this 59-year-old, aligning with leukemoid reaction, or does the chronicity of constitutional symptoms counter this mechanism?", "result_summary": "Chronic symptoms (weight loss over time) contradict reactive leukemoid reaction caused by acute viral illness. While viral infections can cause transient reactive leukocytosis, the prolonged constitutional symptoms suggest myeloproliferative rather than reactive etiology.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "moderate_against"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the physical exam being otherwise normal (no splenomegaly/hepatomegaly) in a 59-year-old with leukocytosis weaken the case for chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), or is splenomegaly commonly absent in early phase presentation?", "result_summary": "Normal exam without splenomegaly doesn't exclude CML, which can present without abdominal organomegaly in early phases. Constitutional symptoms alone justify the current branch assignment, though splenomegaly would strengthen it clinically.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_for", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.7058823529411764, "B2": 0.196078431372549, "B3": 0.0980392156862745, "B1.1": 0.49411764705882344, "B1.2": 0.2117647058823529}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termina