rue, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E26", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum glucose: 120 mg/dL", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E27", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E28", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum calcium: 10.9 mEq/L (corrected for hyperalbuminemia)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E29", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum AST: 12 U/L (within normal range)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E30", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum ALT: 10 U/L (within normal range)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'leukemia', 'leukocytosis', 'hepatomegaly'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=50%, source=both]\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia in Chronic Phase vs Myeloproliferative Neoplasm Not Otherwise Specified (MPN-NOS):\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, bleeding easily, blurred vision, bone pain, enlarged spleen\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Chronic Phase: myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative disease, myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasm\n\nRelated to Myeloproliferative Neoplasm Not Otherwise Specified (MPN-NOS): bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\n[RAG context for 'A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: Leukocytosis. > Prognosis\n  Leukocytosis secondary to benign, nonmalignant, and treatable conditions generally carries a favorable prognosis. In contrast, leukocytosis associated with malignant lymphoproliferative disorders and \n[RAG context for 'A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: Chronic myelogenous leukemia and exposure to ionizing radiation--a retrospective study of 443 patients. > Differential Diagnosis\n  Differentiating CML from other causes of granulocytic leukocytoses, such as infections or drugs, is necessary. Most causes of reactive leukocytosis have a white blood cell count of less than 50 x 10/L\n[RAG context for 'In general' (score=0.53):]\n  Source: Leukocytosis. > Prognosis\n  Leukocytosis secondary to benign, nonmalignant, and treatable conditions generally carries a favorable prognosis. In contrast, leukocytosis associated with malignant lymphoproliferative disorders and \n[RAG context for 'In general' (score=0.54):]\n  Source: The 2016 revision to the World Health Organization classification of myeloid neoplasms and acute leukemia. > History and Physical\n  Chronic leukemia subtypes occur almost exclusively in adults. Many patients are asymptomatic at the time of diagnosis, identified only incidentally after marked leukocytosis is discovered on a complet\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  recent weight loss → anorexia as a constitutional symptom of myeloproliferative neoplasm → Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Chronic Phase\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for systemic symptoms including anorexia as a possible manifestation of CML-CP contributing to weight loss\n  recent weight loss → bone marrow hyperplasia with fibrosis → Myeloproliferative Neoplasm Not Otherwise Specified (MPN-NOS)\n    (frequency: Rare)\n    Suggestion: Order bone marrow biopsy to assess for fibrotic changes indicating MPN-NOS despite absence of classic symptoms\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
