{"case_id": "MB_9", "timestep": 3, "case_summary": "A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue. In general, he has been in good health; however, he recently has experienced weight loss, abdominal pain, and general fatigue. He has a medical history of anxiety, diabetes, a fracture of his foot sustained when he tripped, and a recent cold that caused him to miss work for 1 week. His current medications include metformin, insulin, buspirone, vitamin D, calcium, and sodium docusate. His temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 150/100 mmHg, pulse is 90/min, respirations are 18/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam reveals a calm gentleman. A mild systolic murmur is heard in the left upper sternal region. The rest of the physical exam is within normal limits. Laboratory values are ordered as seen below. Hemoglobin: 12 g/dL Hematocrit: 36% Leukocyte count: 66,500/mm^3 with normal differential Platelet count: 177,000/mm^3 Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase: elevated Serum: Na+: 139 mEq/L Cl-: 100 mEq/L K+: 4.3 mEq/L BUN: 20 mg/dL Glucose: 120 mg/dL Creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL Ca2+: 10.9 mEq/L AST: 12 U/L ALT: 10 U/L Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n[Answer options redacted — use clinical findings only]", "root": {"label": "Chronic Progressive Constitutional Syndrome (Fatigue, Weight Loss, Abdominal Pain) with Elevated Leukocyte Count and Alkaline Phatase", "time_course": "chronic", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.65, "supporting_facts": ["Fatigue, abdominal pain, and weight loss as primary constitutional symptoms", "Leukocyte count: 66,500/mm³ with normal differential (atypical leukocytosis for infectious aetiology)", "Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase: elevated (critical Tier 2 marker for myeloproliferative processes)", "Mild systolic murmur in left upper sternal region (cardiac involvement is plausible but unproven)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Myeloproliferative neoplasm (elevated white count with normal differential aligns with CML chronic phase but lacks blasts)", "Reactive leukemoid reaction (could explain elevated count but no evidence of chronic constitutional symptoms in this context)", "Atypical infection with granulocytic response (high WBC + normal differential is less typical than sepsis profiles)"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional features (weight loss, fatigue)", "Elevated WBC count (66.5 × 10⁹/L) with no neutrophilic predominance", "Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase elevation (Tier 2 high-risk evidence item)", "Mild hypertension (150/100 mmHg)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.7058823529411764, "posterior": 0.4002998500749625, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Elevated LAP with normal differential (no blasts) is a key point favoring CML over reactive leukemoid reaction. CML classically demonstrates low LAP (Ph-negative) with blast phase showing increased blasts; reactive causes have elevated LAP.", "Normal exam without splenomegaly doesn't exclude CML, which can present without abdominal organomegaly in early phases. Constitutional symptoms alone justify the current branch assignment, though splenomegaly would strengthen it clinically."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of gradual blood count abnormalities", "Presence of splenomegaly or hepatomegaly"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Bone marrow biopsy with cytogenetics"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.9, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.196078431372549, "posterior": 0.09995002498750626, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Normal differential (no lymphocytosis) reduces the likelihood of CLL or lymphoplasia, though MM remains plausible with non-specific leukocytosis. Lymphocytosis is more directly characteristic of lymphoproliferative disorders."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of lymphadenopathy or monoclonal protein symptoms"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Flow cytometry for clonal surface markers", "Serum protein electrophoresis"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "severitity"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "prior": 0.0980392156862745, "posterior": 0.04997501249375313, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP with normal differential (no blasts) is a key point favoring CML over reactive leukemoid reaction. CML classically demonstrates low LAP (Ph-negative) with blast phase showing increased blasts; reactive causes have elevated LAP.", "Chronic symptoms (weight loss over time) contradict reactive leukemoid reaction caused by acute viral illness. While viral infections can cause transient reactive leukocytosis, the prolonged constitutional symptoms suggest myeloproliferative rather than reactive etiology.", "Elevated LAP in a normal differential excludes CML in chronic phase (characterised by low LAP) and supports myelofibrosis or MPN-NOS over reactive leukocytosis. Lack of blasts directly rules out blast crisis. However, this does not yet confirm MPN-NOS specifically."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Identify potential infections", "Evaluate for myeloproliferative features"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Bone marrow aspirate"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Chronic Phase", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.49411764705882344, "posterior": 0.15742128935532232, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Absence of JAK2/V617F mutations weakens MPN-NOS (strongly associated with PV/ET) but leaves MPN-NOS as plausible if other myeloid criteria are unmet. Normal differential without blasts further supports CML over MPN-NOS."], "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP in a normal differential excludes CML in chronic phase (characterised by low LAP) and supports myelofibrosis or MPN-NOS over reactive leukocytosis. Lack of blasts directly rules out blast crisis. However, this does not yet confirm MPN-NOS specifically.", "Elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) is most inconsistent with CML in chronic phase, which has characteristically low LAP. This strongly supports exclusion of CML as the primary diagnosis in this context."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of progressive leukocytosis without prior myeloproliferative disorder", "Absence of splenomegaly (can occur in early CML chronic phase)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Ph chromosome detection by FISH or molecular testing", "Bone marrow cytochemistry for myeloperoxidase (normal in CML chronic phase)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Myeloproliferative Neoplasm Not Otherwise Specified (MPN-NOS)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "prior": 0.2117647058823529, "posterior": 0.24287856071964015, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Elevated LAP in a normal differential excludes CML in chronic phase (characterised by low LAP) and supports myelofibrosis or MPN-NOS over reactive leukocytosis. Lack of blasts directly rules out blast crisis. However, this does not yet confirm MPN-NOS specifically.", "Absence of organomegaly does not exclude MPN-NOS as per WHO criteria, which permits this diagnosis without spleenomegaly. However, it slightly weakens CML's candidacy since splenomegaly is a key sign but can be absent in early disease."], "evidence_against": ["Absence of JAK2/V617F mutations weakens MPN-NOS (strongly associated with PV/ET) but leaves MPN-NOS as plausible if other myeloid criteria are unmet. Normal differential without blasts further supports CML over MPN-NOS."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Absence of JAK2/V617F mutation (more common in PV/ET)", "Normal red cell mass (contrasts with PV)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Bone marrow biopsy for fibrosis (excludes myelofibrosis)", "CSF3R mutation testing"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology"}}, "frontier": ["B2", "B1.1"], "other_mass": 0.0, "candidate_leaves": [{"leaf_id": "B1.1::ANALYZE_VIGNETTE::0", "branch_id": "B1.1", "leaf_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "In a 59-year-old with constitutional symptoms and elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP), does the combination of normal differential (no blasts) and chronic course provide conclusive support for CML in chronic phase over MPN-NOS or reactive processes?", "expected_information_gain": 