 abdominal swelling, bleeding easily, blurred vision, bone pain, enlarged spleen\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Phase Transformation (CML in Blast Crisis) vs Myeloproliferative Neoplasm, Not Otherwise Specified (MPN-NOS):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Chronic Phase: myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative disease, myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasm\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Phase Transformation (CML in Blast Crisis): cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Myeloproliferative Neoplasm, Not Otherwise Specified (MPN-NOS): bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Phase Transformation (CML in Blast Crisis)"}
